1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:02,120
Imagine looking inside the brain of a modern

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:04,660
artificial intelligence right at the moment it learns

3
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:07,570
something new. On the screen, picture a massive

4
00:00:07,570 --> 00:00:10,320
network of glowing nodes arranged in tall vertical

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:13,550
columns from left to right, connected by thousands

6
00:00:13,550 --> 00:00:16,000
of thin pulsing lines. Each line has a

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,910
tunable dial, a weight that controls how strongly

8
00:00:18,910 --> 00:00:22,410
one neuron influences the next. When you show

9
00:00:22,410 --> 00:00:24,290
this network an image, a wave of numbers

10
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:26,090
flows from the input column on the far

11
00:00:26,090 --> 00:00:27,960
left, all the way to the output column

12
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:30,080
on the far right, where the network makes

13
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:32,760
a guess. Early in training, that guess is

14
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:34,700
usually wrong. At the very end of the

15
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:36,940
network, a single number lights up in red,

16
00:00:36,940 --> 00:00:39,600
measuring the size of the mistake. We call

17
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:42,320
this the loss. Now, here is the central

18
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:45,100
mystery of deep learning. Buried all the way

19
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:46,950
back in the very first column on the

20
00:00:46,950 --> 00:00:48,920
left is a single dial. To make the

21
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:51,250
network smarter, we need to turn that dial

22
00:00:51,250 --> 00:00:53,610
slightly up or slightly down to shrink the

23
00:00:53,610 --> 00:00:55,760
red error score at the far right. If

24
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:57,800
that dial does not touch the final output

25
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:01,300
directly, its influence passes through dozens of intermediate

26
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:04,430
layers, getting added, multiplied and bent through curve

27
00:01:04,430 --> 00:01:07,010
after curve along the way. How could that

28
00:01:07,010 --> 00:01:08,900
one tiny dial at the beginning possibly know

29
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:11,180
how much it contributed to the final mistake

30
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:13,470
at the end? The answer, and the mathematical

31
00:01:13,470 --> 00:01:16,720
engine behind virtually all of modern artificial intelligence,

32
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:19,560
is a single, remarkably simple idea from first

33
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:22,480
year calculus. It is called the chain rule.

34
00:01:22,540 --> 00:01:25,160
In Rome Shroom, Combs Pawnode, most people first

35
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:26,920
meet the chain rule as a dry rule

36
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:29,800
for shuffling symbols on a page, memorize the

37
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:32,660
formula, pass the test and forget it. But

38
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:34,520
if you look at it visually, the chain

39
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:36,680
rule is not really about algebra at all.

40
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:38,730
It is the mathematics of cause and effect

41
00:01:38,730 --> 00:01:41,380
flowing through a chain of connected systems. And

42
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:43,480
once you see what it actually looks like,

43
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:46,570
both calculus and neural networks suddenly click into

44
00:01:46,570 --> 00:01:48,700
place. To see why, let us clear the

45
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:51,040
complex network from the screen and start with

46
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:54,110
the simplest possible visual building block. What a

47
00:01:54,110 --> 00:01:56,840
derivative actually looks like when you strip away

48
00:01:56,840 --> 00:01:59,600
the jargon. Instead of drawing a traditional coordinate

49
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:01,160
grid with a curved line and a tangent

50
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:04,380
slope, let us try a different picture. Imagine

51
00:02:04,380 --> 00:02:06,720
two horizontal number lines floating on a dark

52
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:09,450
background. One stacked directly above the other. On

53
00:02:09,450 --> 00:02:11,610
the top number line sits a glowing blue

54
00:02:11,610 --> 00:02:15,030
slider. This represents our input variable, which we

55
00:02:15,030 --> 00:02:17,240
will call X. On the bottom number line

56
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:20,120
sits a glowing yellow slider. This represents our

57
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:22,920
output variable, which we will call Y. Suppose

58
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:24,970
these two sliders are linked by a simple

59
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:28,670
rule. Y equals X squared. Whenever you drag

60
00:02:28,670 --> 00:02:30,630
the blue slider along the top line, the

61
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:33,640
yellow slider automatically moves to the matching squared

62
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:35,890
value on the bottom line. Let us place

63
00:02:35,890 --> 00:02:38,240
the blue slider right at the number three.

64
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:40,820
Because three squared is nine. The yellow slider

65
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:42,830
on the bottom line settles right at the

66
00:02:42,830 --> 00:02:45,540
number nine. Now, watch what happens when we

67
00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:47,480
give the blue slider a tiny nudge to

68
00:02:47,480 --> 00:02:50,280
the right. Watch a small green arrow pushing

69
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:52,100
X forward by a tiny fraction of a

70
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:54,820
unit. In calculus, we call the length of

71
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:57,310
that tiny input nudge DX. The moment X

72
00:02:57,310 --> 00:02:59,450
nudge is forward, the yellow slider on the

73
00:02:59,450 --> 00:03:01,580
bottom line responds with its own nudge to

74
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:04,400
the right, which we call DY. Because we

75
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:06,080
are sitting near X equals three on a

76
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:09,330
squaring function. That bottom nudge is noticeably wider

77
00:03:09,330 --> 00:03:11,990
than the top nudge. Specifically, if you zoom

78
00:03:11,990 --> 00:03:14,800
in closely around this spot, the bottom arrow

79
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:17,520
DY is almost exactly six times as wide

80
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:20,670
as the top arrow DX. That magnification factor,

81
00:03:20,670 --> 00:03:22,860
how much the output nudge gets stretched or

82
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:25,120
shrunk compared to the input nudge, is the

83
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:28,020
entire meaning of a derivative. When we write

84
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:30,780
DY over DX, we are simply asking, if

85
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:32,720
I wiggle the top slider by a tiny

86
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:35,220
amount, by what factor does that wiggle get

87
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:38,300
amplified on the bottom slider? For the function

88
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:40,750
Y equals X squared, calculus tells us the

89
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:43,890
derivative is two times X. And X equals

90
00:03:43,890 --> 00:03:46,610
three, two times three is six. So any

91
00:03:46,610 --> 00:03:49,590
tiny input nudge gets magnified by a factor

92
00:03:49,590 --> 00:03:52,310
of six, hoof. With that visual anchor in

93
00:03:52,310 --> 00:03:54,560
place, we are ready to build a chain.

94
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:56,620
What happens when we add a third horizontal

95
00:03:56,620 --> 00:03:58,320
number line at the very bottom of the

96
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:01,050
screen? Let us place a glowing red slider

97
00:04:01,050 --> 00:04:03,320
on this third line and call it Z.

98
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:05,480
Now we have a three level cascade stacked

99
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:07,580
from top to bottom. The blue slider X

100
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:09,640
at the top controls the yellow slider Y

101
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:11,850
in the middle, and the yellow slider Y

102
00:04:11,850 --> 00:04:14,300
in the middle controls the red slider Z

103
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:16,770
at the bottom. Before we put any complicated

104
00:04:16,770 --> 00:04:19,280
curves into this system, imagine these three sliders

105
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:22,530
are linked like simple mechanical gears. Suppose that

106
00:04:22,530 --> 00:04:24,370
whenever we nudge the top slider X, the

107
00:04:24,370 --> 00:04:26,790
middle slider Y moves twice as far, in

108
00:04:26,790 --> 00:04:30,240
the language of derivatives, the scaling factor from

109
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:32,720
X to Y, written as DY over DX

110
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:35,840
equals two. At the same time, suppose that

111
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:38,300
whenever the middle slider Y moves, the bottom

112
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:41,110
slider Z moves three times as far as

113
00:04:41,110 --> 00:04:42,040
Y did. That means the scaling factor from

114
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:42,610
Y to Z, written as DZ over DY

115
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:50,320
equals three. Now ask yourself the natural question.

116
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:52,000
If we give the top slider X a

117
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,560
tiny nudge DX, how much does the bottom

118
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:57,500
slider Z move in response? Let us watch

119
00:04:57,500 --> 00:04:59,810
the nudge travel down the screen step by

120
00:04:59,810 --> 00:05:02,030
step. First, the tiny arrow DX on the

121
00:05:02,030 --> 00:05:04,200
top line drops down to the middle line,

122
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:06,920
where it gets stretched to twice its original

123
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:10,340
length. That new doubled arrow is DY. Next,

124
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:12,290
that middle arrow drops down to the bottom

125
00:05:12,290 --> 00:05:14,360
line, where it gets stretched by a factor

126
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:17,360
of three. An arrow that was first multiplied

127
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:19,680
by two, and then multiplied by three, ends

128
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:21,640
up six times as long as the original

129
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:24,110
nudge at the top. Two multiplied by three

130
00:05:24,110 --> 00:05:27,140
equals six. That is the chain rule. Visually,

131
00:05:27,140 --> 00:05:29,170
if one system magnifies a nudge by a

132
00:05:29,170 --> 00:05:32,170
first factor, and a second system magnifies that

133
00:05:32,170 --> 00:05:35,050
result by a second factor, the total magnification

134
00:05:35,050 --> 00:05:37,480
from start to finish is simply the product

135
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:39,790
of the two factors. Written out as an

136
00:05:39,790 --> 00:05:42,220
equation, the derivative of Z with respect to

137
00:05:42,220 --> 00:05:44,790
X equals the derivative of Z with respect

138
00:05:44,790 --> 00:05:47,690
to Y, multiplied by the derivative of Y

139
00:05:47,690 --> 00:05:50,860
with respect to X, or in fraction form,

140
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:54,910
DZ over DX equals DZ over DY times

141
00:05:54,910 --> 00:05:57,700
DY over DX. When you see those two

142
00:05:57,700 --> 00:05:59,600
ratios side by side on the screen, it

143
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:02,420
looks almost irresistible to cross out the matching

144
00:06:02,420 --> 00:06:04,840
DY terms in the numerator and the denominator,

145
00:06:04,840 --> 00:06:07,840
as if they were ordinary fractions cancelling out.

146
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:10,190
In many classrooms, you are strictly warned never

147
00:06:10,190 --> 00:06:12,360
to think of it that way, because derivatives

148
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:15,300
are formally defined as limits rather than static

149
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:18,570
fractions. Yet when you picture derivatives as actual,

150
00:06:18,570 --> 00:06:21,440
tiny nudges on stacked number lines, before shrinking

151
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:23,820
them down towards zero, the middle change DY

152
00:06:23,820 --> 00:06:26,860
literally is the same intermediate movement in both

153
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:29,210
ratios. It is the output of the first

154
00:06:29,210 --> 00:06:31,680
step, and the input to the second step.

155
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:34,030
Knowing the two ratios works precisely because the

156
00:06:34,030 --> 00:06:36,090
middle nudge bridges the top line and the

157
00:06:36,090 --> 00:06:38,250
bottom line. Now let us see how this

158
00:06:38,250 --> 00:06:40,880
visual chain handles a genuine non-linear function that

159
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:44,120
looks intimidating on paper. Let us place the

160
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:46,680
equation Z equals the sine of X squared

161
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:48,800
at the top of the screen. If you

162
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:50,680
try to picture the graph of the sine

163
00:06:50,680 --> 00:06:52,370
of X squared all at once, you see

164
00:06:52,370 --> 00:06:54,990
a wave that oscillates faster and faster as

165
00:06:54,990 --> 00:06:57,480
you move away from zero, finding the slope

166
00:06:57,480 --> 00:07:01,000
of that compressed wave directly feels daunting. Instead,

167
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,120
let us break the formula apart, and feed

168
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:06,040
it into our three stacked number lines. On

169
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:08,920
the top line sits our input slider, X.

170
00:07:08,920 --> 00:07:10,600
To get from the top line to the

171
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:12,900
middle line Y, we apply the inside part

172
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:16,390
of the formula, Y equals X squared. Then,

173
00:07:16,390 --> 00:07:18,040
to get from the middle line Y to

174
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:20,240
the bottom line Z, we apply the outside

175
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:23,040
part of the formula, Z equals the sine

176
00:07:23,040 --> 00:07:25,640
of Y, look at how clean that is.

177
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:28,020
Neither step is complicated on its own. The

178
00:07:28,020 --> 00:07:30,120
first step is just a simple parabola, and

179
00:07:30,120 --> 00:07:32,080
the second step is just a standard sine

180
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:34,540
wave. Now let us send a tiny nudge

181
00:07:34,540 --> 00:07:36,620
DX into the top slider, and watch how

182
00:07:36,620 --> 00:07:39,280
it scales as it falls through both steps.

183
00:07:39,280 --> 00:07:41,140
As the nudge drops from the top line

184
00:07:41,140 --> 00:07:42,880
X to the middle line Y, it passes

185
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:47,320
through the squaring rule, Y equals X squared.

186
00:07:47,320 --> 00:07:49,700
We already know that the local scaling factor

187
00:07:49,700 --> 00:07:51,300
for X squared is 2 times X, so

188
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:54,000
on the middle line, the arrow DY has

189
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,160
a length of 2 times X multiplied by

190
00:07:57,160 --> 00:07:59,920
DX. Next, that middle arrow DY triggers a

191
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:02,680
movement on the bottom line Z, passing through

192
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:05,640
the rule Z equals the sine of Y.

193
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:08,370
From basic calculus, the scaling factor for a

194
00:08:08,370 --> 00:08:10,050
sine wave at any point Y is the

195
00:08:10,050 --> 00:08:12,950
cosine of Y, so whatever nudge arrives at

196
00:08:12,950 --> 00:08:15,350
the middle line gets multiplied by the cosine

197
00:08:15,350 --> 00:08:17,040
of Y as it drops to the bottom

198
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:19,800
line. But the two steps together, the initial

199
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:22,200
nudge DX first gets multiplied by 2 times

200
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:24,910
X, and then gets multiplied by the cosine

201
00:08:24,910 --> 00:08:27,560
of Y, therefore the overall scaling factor from

202
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:30,520
top to bottom, DZ over DX, is simply

203
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:33,600
2 times X multiplied by the cosine of

204
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:36,160
Y. Finally, to write our answer purely in

205
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:38,520
terms of our original input X, we remember

206
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:40,720
that the middle slider Y is, sits at

207
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:43,600
the position X squared, replacing Y with X

208
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:47,110
squared gives us our complete derivative, 2 times

209
00:08:47,110 --> 00:08:50,080
X multiplied by the cosine of X squared.

210
00:08:50,080 --> 00:08:52,280
Every time you hear the classic textbook phrase,

211
00:08:52,280 --> 00:08:55,060
take the derivative of the outside function, leave

212
00:08:55,060 --> 00:08:57,600
the inside alone, and multiply by the derivative

213
00:08:57,600 --> 00:09:00,390
of the inside function. This three line cascade

214
00:09:00,390 --> 00:09:02,960
is what is actually happening. You keep the

215
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:05,650
inside function inside the cosine, because the bottom

216
00:09:05,650 --> 00:09:08,520
slider's sensitivity depends on where the middle slider

217
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:10,720
is currently sitting, and you multiply by the

218
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:13,250
derivative of the inside function, because the middle

219
00:09:13,250 --> 00:09:15,840
slider's movement was already stretched by that exact

220
00:09:15,840 --> 00:09:18,640
factor, before it ever reached the final step.

221
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:20,900
Now, hold that image of the three stacked

222
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:23,120
number lines in your mind, and watch as

223
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:25,890
we rotate them 90 degrees, so they stand

224
00:09:25,890 --> 00:09:28,520
vertically from left to right. Let us add

225
00:09:28,520 --> 00:09:31,040
a fourth vertical line, then a fifth, then

226
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:34,490
dozens more, turning our simple three-step chain into

227
00:09:34,490 --> 00:09:37,370
a long corridor of linked sliders. Around each

228
00:09:37,370 --> 00:09:39,240
line, let us bring back the glowing nodes

229
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:41,500
and connecting lines from the beginning of the

230
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:43,980
video. Just like that, we are back inside

231
00:09:43,980 --> 00:09:47,210
our artificial intelligence network. A deep neural network

232
00:09:47,210 --> 00:09:49,890
is nothing more than a massive composite function,

233
00:09:49,890 --> 00:09:52,910
a long chain of simple mathematical steps feeding

234
00:09:52,910 --> 00:09:54,980
one into the next. Let us highlight a

235
00:09:54,980 --> 00:09:56,910
single path glowing in gold from the first

236
00:09:56,910 --> 00:09:58,800
column on the left to the final error

237
00:09:58,800 --> 00:10:01,200
score on the right. At the very beginning

238
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:03,240
of this path is our single tunable dial,

239
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:05,100
a weight we will call W, when we

240
00:10:05,100 --> 00:10:07,640
nudge that dial by a tiny amount DW,

241
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:09,960
it nudges the value of a neuron in

242
00:10:10,020 --> 00:10:13,200
the first layer, which we will call A1.

243
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:15,400
At tiny shift in A1 nudges are connected

244
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:17,640
neuron in the second layer, which we will

245
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:20,970
call A2. At shift in A2 nudges a

246
00:10:20,970 --> 00:10:24,320
neuron in the third layer, A3. And finally,

247
00:10:24,320 --> 00:10:26,560
at shift changes the red error score at

248
00:10:26,560 --> 00:10:29,050
the end, the loss L. Remember our original

249
00:10:29,050 --> 00:10:31,160
question, how does the computer know how to

250
00:10:31,160 --> 00:10:33,800
adjust that very first dial W to reduce

251
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:36,760
the final error L? In calculus terms, the

252
00:10:36,760 --> 00:10:39,480
computer needs to know the total sensitivity connecting

253
00:10:39,480 --> 00:10:42,110
the start of the chain to the end

254
00:10:42,110 --> 00:10:45,090
of the chain, DL over DW. Thanks to

255
00:10:45,090 --> 00:10:47,250
the chain rule, the computer never has to

256
00:10:47,250 --> 00:10:49,940
solve one giant impossible equation for the whole

257
00:10:49,940 --> 00:10:53,140
network. Instead, it breaks the problem into tiny

258
00:10:53,140 --> 00:10:57,130
local steps along the path. The total sensitivity

259
00:10:57,130 --> 00:11:00,790
DL over DW is simply DL over DA3,

260
00:11:00,790 --> 00:11:04,730
multiplied by DA3 over DA2, multiplied by DA2

261
00:11:04,730 --> 00:11:09,390
over DA1, multiplied by DA1 over DW. When

262
00:11:09,390 --> 00:11:12,230
an artificial intelligence trains on data, an algorithm

263
00:11:12,230 --> 00:11:14,140
starts at the final error on the right

264
00:11:14,140 --> 00:11:16,580
side of the screen and walks backward toward

265
00:11:16,580 --> 00:11:18,920
the left, layer by layer. At each step,

266
00:11:18,920 --> 00:11:21,260
it measures the local scaling factor between two

267
00:11:21,260 --> 00:11:24,230
neighboring columns, and multiplies it into a running

268
00:11:24,230 --> 00:11:26,630
total. By the time that backward wave reaches

269
00:11:26,630 --> 00:11:28,960
the first layer, every single weight knows its

270
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:32,220
exact magnification factor, relative to the final mistake,

271
00:11:32,220 --> 00:11:34,750
and nudges itself in the opposite direction to

272
00:11:34,750 --> 00:11:37,750
make the error smaller. Computer scientists call this

273
00:11:37,750 --> 00:11:40,720
backward walk back propagation, but underneath the impressive

274
00:11:40,720 --> 00:11:43,080
name, it is purely the chain rule running

275
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:46,520
in reverse across millions of sliders at once.

276
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:48,110
So the next time you see an intricate

277
00:11:48,110 --> 00:11:50,320
derivative in a calculus textbook, or watch an

278
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:53,900
artificial intelligence generate human language and art, look

279
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:56,950
past the surface complexity. Underneath it all is

280
00:11:56,950 --> 00:11:59,880
the same gentle geometry, a single tiny nudge,

281
00:11:59,880 --> 00:12:02,520
rippling through a chain of connected spaces, scaling

282
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:04,040
step by step along the way.

